  "storySettings": {
    "worldBackground": "GENRE: colourful military space opera and adult academy life. Vanguard Joint Expeditionary Academy is a multinational, multispecies officer university aboard Vanguard Station in the Sol system. Every cadet is an adult. The academy trains Expeditionary Combat Officers who must qualify both as aerospace combat pilots and as ground/boarding officers: fly, navigate, fight, survive EVA, lead small units, board ships, and continue missions after plans collapse.\n\nHumanity entered interstellar civilization only a few decades ago after the Concord of Associated Worlds uplifted Earth with jump travel, fusion power, advanced medicine, inertial control, and mature space infrastructure. Humans have adapted unnervingly fast. Terran pilots, engineers, and expeditionary troops already have a reputation for improvisation, aggression, and modifying technology beyond conservative alien tolerances. Older Concord peoples variously admire, patronize, desire, distrust, or fear the newcomers.\n\nThe Concord is a pluralistic alliance rather than a spotless utopia. Its principal strategic rival is the Varkesh Hegemony, an old imperial star state dominated politically by the reptilian/draconic Kharad. The Hegemony sees uplift as Concord expansion by proxy and Earth as a rapidly militarizing client power. Kharad species identity is not Hegemony loyalty: reformists, refugees, breakaway colonies and exiles exist, including a tiny number at Vanguard. The authoritarian Orsine Dominion cooperates with Varkesh interests when useful. Neutral systems, client states, pirates, corporations, and worlds trying to avoid choosing sides complicate the frontier. A second developing civilization, the short, empathic and caregiving-centered Astrai, is now being cautiously uplifted, forcing humans to confront the ethics of the process from the powerful side. Other allied peoples include feline Veyari, horned high-gravity Tesh, amphibious Naelori, sensory-specialist Ilyri and practical predatory plantfolk Verdari.\n\nVanguard is still a university. Cadets attend classes, fail exams, train, compete, drink, date, flirt, form queer and straight relationships, argue with roommates, visit Earth on leave, and build identities inside permanent six-person Flights. The player's Flight is the emotional core of play. Romance and attraction are normal adult life, but consent, fraternization rules, and command authority remain distinct. Instructor-cadet relationships are prohibited while direct authority exists.\n\nThe war begins as distant geopolitical pressure. It escalates through academy time rather than arbitrary narrator interruptions: traffic rises, live weapons appear, instructors receive fleet orders, leave contracts, refugees and casualty lists arrive, and senior Flights vanish into provisional commissions. Away missions begin as supervised practicums, then encounter real raiders, listening posts, border incidents, boarding actions, and eventually operations that are training in name only. Quiet life remains valid play until established causes intrude.\n\nVanguard Flights are persistent training-and-deployment groups. A standard Flight is nominally six cadets, but multiplayer PCs are never split: every PC in the room is assigned to the same Flight and occupies a seat before NPC cadets are chosen. NPCs fill only remaining vacancies and the established roster persists unless play explicitly changes it.",
    "questGenerationGuidance": "## Academy-first play\nGenerate at most one new quest per turn. Prefer existing instructors, ACTIVE Flight members, academy offices, locations, factions, and current courses. Never treat every candidate-pool cadet as a member of the player Flight. Do not invent a new town, station, planet, or instructor merely to create a quest. Classes and routine training are not quests; the Academic Ledger owns attendance and qualification continuity.\n\n## Quest grammar\nAcademy quests: remediation, competition, equipment trouble, disciplinary obligations, favors, club or dorm problems, relationship repair, research, practical evaluations, and Flight responsibilities. Away-mission quests: reconnaissance, rescue, escort, survival, boarding, first-contact security, recovery of people or materiel, and combined aerospace/ground operations. War quests emerge from established campaign pressure rather than random attacks.\n\n## Scale\nEarly problems are local and personal. Frontier danger rises gradually. Do not hand a first-term cadet strategic command or galaxy-saving destiny. Existing military institutions provide orders, restrictions, consequences, and opportunities without taking away the player's choice of method.\n\n## Refusal and failure\nOptional work can be refused, delayed, failed, or solved partially. Mandatory academy practicums remain institutional obligations; refusal creates academic or disciplinary consequences rather than invisible railroading.\n\n## Relationships\nNever make every attractive NPC a romance quest. Relationships grow through direct evidence: shared work, trust, humor, competence, risk, honesty, boundaries, attraction, conflict, repair, and chosen intimacy.\n\n## Vanguard Academy Activity Discipline\nCore qualifications and ordered practicums are institutional duties, but optional sports, culture nights, clubs, dates and social events are not quests unless the player chooses to make a goal of them. Offer at most one new quest per turn. Prefer existing instructors, rival Flights, academy areas and authored practicums before inventing new teachers or map pins. Mandatory mission orders should state the reporting time, roster and objective; refusal or delay produces plausible military consequences rather than invisible teleportation.\n\n## Optional story lanes\nUse Wings & Rivalries, Mud, Vacuum & Steel, Academy Life, Hearts in Uniform, Uplift Generation and The Kharad Question as available lanes, not simultaneous quest quotas. Generate a quest only when the player has actually engaged a lane strongly enough to create a concrete goal. Marquee calendar lead-in may produce invitations or preparation, but an event listing is not a quest and a live window is not acceptance. Let ordinary encounter play carry much of academy life without turning every recurring activity into journal work.\n\n\n## Destination, deadline, and completion honesty\nUse existing named realms, regions, locations and areas first. State the actual destination when the giver reasonably knows it; if coordinates/location are uncertain, say what is known rather than inventing a new map pin. Deadlines must allow plausible preparation, travel, work and return. Discovery, arrival, partial rescue, partial recovery or gathering one clue is progress, not automatic quest completion. Do not create a duplicate station, planet, settlement or training site merely because a generated quest wants scenery.\n\n## Authored Theatre Density\n- The theatre now contains many authored destinations. Prefer an existing named location and one of its authored areas before creating a new station, colony, base, port, relay, hospital, range or social venue.\n- A generated quest must name the actual destination it uses and account for travel time. Do not add a new map pin merely to provide scenery.\n- Use the location's function: hospitals create treatment/recovery work; ports create transit/customs/logistics; ranges create training; civilian places create ordinary social/economic problems; frontier sites create rescue, intelligence, patrol, trade or political pressure."
  },
