      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Ghost> Ghost gives Ryn's head one last soft pat between the ears.\n\nGhost: [low, dry] We'll see. First we need to find out how room arrangements work here. Also, you're pretty trusting for someone who just met me. But you'll get your head scratchies.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ghost> He turns back to his terminal, wedge diagram squared and facing the aisle, and lets the class have the rest of him.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Commander Seraphine Kade> Kade walks the aisle at a measured pace, boots clicking, and stops directly in front of the back row, her gaze sweeping the two nearest terminals.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ryn Valea> Ryn's ears drop flat the instant Kade's shadow falls across her desk. Her hand slides onto the screen's edge, angling it a few degrees away from the Commander's line of sight while she throws Ghost a wide-eyed, pleading look.\n\nRyn Valea: [whispering, urgent] Honey. Honey. Fix it with your eyes. Do something.\n\nGhost: [flat, quiet] You drew it wrong, kitten. Own it.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Commander Seraphine Kade> Kade reaches out and, without hurry, rotates Ryn's terminal back to face her, studying the wedge for three full seconds.\n\nCommander Kade: [dryly] Cadet Valea. Your trailing wingman on the short side is inside the lead's protection sector. That's not a wedge — that's a trap. If the lead breaks high, you've drawn your worst job into the one place he can't maneuver.\n\nRyn Valea: [hesitant] ...Yes, Commander. I'll correct it.\n\nCommander Kade: [calmly] See that you do. [turning to Ghost, eyes on his diagram] Cadet Rivera.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Commander Seraphine Kade> She reads his wedge top to bottom, protection sectors shaded, short-side trailing wingman correctly outside the lead's coverage, vectors labeled in a clean, economical hand.\n\nCommander Kade: [dryly, after a pause] Correct. Labeling's clean, sectors are right, and your worst-job answer is implied in the geometry itself — he's outside the lead's cover and inside the enemy's first firing solution. [straightening] I'd ask why you didn't tell your Flightmate her drawing was wrong before I got here, but I suspect I already know the answer.\n\nGhost: [level] Yes, Commander. She'd have learned less.\n\nCommander Kade: [dryly] Or she'd have learned more and embarrassed herself less. Both lessons count. [moving on down the row] Amara.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Amara Okoye> Amara's diagram sits dead center, perfectly to spec. Kade studies it for a beat, gives a single nod, and continues up the aisle without comment.\n\nAmara Okoye: [murmuring, eyes forward] One of us can follow instructions. Noted for the record.\n\nRyn Valea: [whispering, sulking] Traitor. Both of you.",
